---
const problems = [
  {
    title: 'Staffing gaps',
    body: 'Unfilled positions turn into closed amenities, missed coverage, and calls to the office.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Last-minute callouts',
    body: 'Someone calling out at 8am should not become your whole morning.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Day-to-day operations',
    body: 'Opening, closing, supplies, incident reports, and the small things that add up.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Resident expectations',
    body: 'Residents notice when something is off, and the board hears about it before you do.',
  },
];
---

<section class="section border-b border-border">
  <div class="container-page">
    <span class="eyebrow">Community operations</span>

    <h2
      class="mt-5 max-w-[18ch] text-[32px] font-bold leading-[1.05] tracking-[-0.03em] md:text-[40px] lg:text-[48px]"
    >
      There are enough things to manage already.
    </h2>

    <p class="mt-6 max-w-[62ch] text-[18px] leading-[1.65] text-muted lg:text-[20px]">
      Running a community is not one job. It is staffing, scheduling, coverage,
      certifications, daily operations, and constant communication with
      residents and boards. Most of that lands on people who already have a full
      portfolio to run, and none of it should be something you have to chase
      every week.
    </p>

    <ul class="mt-14 grid gap-x-10 gap-y-10 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:mt-20 lg:grid-cols-4">
      {
        problems.map((problem) => (
          <li class="border-t border-border pt-5">
            <h3 class="text-[18px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em]">
              {problem.title}
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-2.5 text-[16px] leading-[1.6] text-muted">{problem.body}</p>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>
